86761953 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761939} :step/id #uuid "ea102e57-2d17-5036-b8c6-56b734a6ca43" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761922}}] :activity/title "MTH 256 — R-7: Quiz 3 Review" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045461} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761936}] [:step/id #uuid "bae418df-3fb7-58ac-90b7-b6636ae4a95a" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761940 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761941} :step/id #uuid "bae418df-3fb7-58ac-90b7-b6636ae4a95a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761908}}] [:step/id #uuid "e2fe0097-7488-58fb-9b59-6c23734a19b3" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761951 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761910} :step/id #uuid "e2fe0097-7488-58fb-9b59-6c23734a19b3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761906}}] [:step/id #uuid "ea102e57-2d17-5036-b8c6-56b734a6ca43" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761953 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761939} :step/id #uuid "ea102e57-2d17-5036-b8c6-56b734a6ca43" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761922}}] [:step/id #uuid "0483464d-afc1-59ca-a243-a7d894a83946" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761906 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761943} :step/id #uuid "0483464d-afc1-59ca-a243-a7d894a83946" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761915}}] [:step/id #uuid "0d124e95-43ee-5e2c-9fae-45f0a38b379d" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761908 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761929} :step/id #uuid "0d124e95-43ee-5e2c-9fae-45f0a38b379d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761953}}] [:step/id #uuid "0e5d9fdf-5726-5e35-9b03-7efa9ba91c9c" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761909 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761919} :step/id #uuid "0e5d9fdf-5726-5e35-9b03-7efa9ba91c9c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761911}}] [:step/id #uuid "0ff64869-4264-5def-81ec-54f44f853149" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761911 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761921} :step/id #uuid "0ff64869-4264-5def-81ec-54f44f853149" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761940}}] [:step/id #uuid "1abe3230-4dea-554c-8f05-5776dcde1a1b" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761915 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761952} :step/id #uuid "1abe3230-4dea-554c-8f05-5776dcde1a1b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761925}}] [:step/id #uuid "20d15ec8-9721-55e4-a249-ad92e3654daa" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761916 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761945} :step/id #uuid "20d15ec8-9721-55e4-a249-ad92e3654daa" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761909}}] [:step/id #uuid "296a779b-ff39-5f0d-8278-3ca5c3910e3a" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761922 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761944} :step/id #uuid "296a779b-ff39-5f0d-8278-3ca5c3910e3a"}] [:step/id #uuid "349f0ba1-7ded-5461-859f-e3003a382d3f" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761925 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761935} :step/id #uuid "349f0ba1-7ded-5461-859f-e3003a382d3f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761916}}] [:tutorial/id #uuid "0f97993b-9e3a-5621-807f-4cb2646d6ab5" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761910 :tutorial/content "**MTH 256 — Recitation 7: Quiz 3 Review**\n\nDue: unknown\nDate imported: 2026-11-13\nSource: [MTH 256 F 2026 Recitation 7 Quiz 3 Review.pdf](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F256%2FR-7%2FMTH%20256%20F%202026%20Recitation%207%20Quiz%203%20Review.pdf)\nAnswer key: [MTH 256 F 2026 Recitation 7 Quiz 3 Review Key.pdf](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F256%2FR-7%2FMTH%20256%20F%202026%20Recitation%207%20Quiz%203%20Review%20Key.pdf)\n\nStudy answers and explanations are authored additions, independently checked against the downloaded key. Problems 1 and 10 contain source inconsistencies; their original wording is preserved and the verified conclusions are explained in the answer reveals. The instructor's intended corrections remain unconfirmed. Neither PDF specifies a due date.\n\n## Instructions\n\nThe recitation this week serves as a review for quiz three, which will be taken in the latter half of recitation time. It also prepares you for the wording on the quiz. The quiz covers Sections 5.1 and 5.2 and Euler equations from Section 7.4. The following definition and theorems will be provided on the quiz (source PDF p. 1).\n\n### Wronskian definition\n\nFor two functions $\\{y_1(x),y_2(x)\\}$,\n$$W(x)=y_1y_2'-y_1'y_2.$$\n\n**Topics:** [Linear Independence of Solutions to Homogeneous ODEs](<file:///home/jake/Developer/study/MA/Mathematical-Analysis-%26-Modeling/DEQ/3.%20Linear%20Differential%20Equations/3.1.%20Theory%20of%20Solutions%20to%20Linear%20ODEs/Lessons/3.1.6.%20Linear%20Independence%20of%20Solutions%20to%20Homogeneous%20ODEs.md>).\n**Prerequisites:** No additional prerequisite links needed.\n\n### Theorem 5.2.1 — Constant coefficients\n\nLet $p(r)=ar^2+br+c$ be the characteristic polynomial of\n$$ay''+by'+cy=0.\\tag{5.2.20}$$\n\n(a) If $p(r)=0$ has distinct real roots $r_1,r_2$, then the general solution is\n$$y=c_1e^{r_1x}+c_2e^{r_2x}.$$\n\n(b) If it has a repeated root $r_1$, then the general solution is\n$$y=e^{r_1x}(c_1+c_2x).$$\n\n(c) If it has complex conjugate roots $r_1=\\lambda+i\\omega$ and $r_2=\\lambda-i\\omega$, where $\\omega>0$, then the general solution is\n$$y=e^{\\lambda x}(c_1\\cos\\omega x+c_2\\sin\\omega x).$$\n\n**Topics:** [Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs: Characteristic Equations With Distinct Real Roots](<file:///home/jake/Developer/study/MA/Mathematical-Analysis-%26-Modeling/DEQ/3.%20Linear%20Differential%20Equations/3.2.%20Second-Order%20Linear%20ODEs%20With%20Constant%20Coefficients/Lessons/3.2.1.%20Second-Order%20Homogeneous%20ODEs-%20Characteristic%20Equations%20With%20Distinct%20Real%20Roots.md>); [Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs: Characteristic Equations With Repeated Roots](<file:///home/jake/Developer/study/MA/Mathematical-Analysis-%26-Modeling/DEQ/3.%20Linear%20Differential%20Equations/3.2.%20Second-Order%20Linear%20ODEs%20With%20Constant%20Coefficients/Lessons/3.2.2.%20Second-Order%20Homogeneous%20ODEs-%20Characteristic%20Equations%20With%20Repeated%20Roots.md>); [Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs: Characteristic Equations With Complex Roots](<file:///home/jake/Developer/study/MA/Mathematical-Analysis-%26-Modeling/DEQ/3.%20Linear%20Differential%20Equations/3.2.%20Second-Order%20Linear%20ODEs%20With%20Constant%20Coefficients/Lessons/3.2.3.%20Second-Order%20Homogeneous%20ODEs-%20Characteristic%20Equations%20With%20Complex%20Roots.md>).\n**Prerequisites:** No additional prerequisite links needed.\n\n### Theorem 7.4.3 — Euler equations\n\nSuppose the roots of the indicial equation\n$$ar(r-1)+br+c=0\\tag{7.4.11}$$\nare $r_1,r_2$. Then the general solution of the Euler equation\n$$ax^2y''+bxy'+cy=0\\tag{7.4.12}$$\non $(0,\\infty)$ is\n\n- $y=c_1x^{r_1}+c_2x^{r_2}$ if $r_1,r_2$ are distinct real numbers;\n- $y=x^{r_1}(c_1+c_2\\ln x)$ if $r_1=r_2$;\n- $y=x^\\lambda[c_1\\cos(\\omega\\ln x)+c_2\\sin(\\omega\\ln x)]$ if $r_1,r_2=\\lambda\\pm i\\omega$ with $\\omega>0$.\n\n**Topics:** [Cauchy-Euler Equations: Characteristic Equations With Distinct Real Roots](<file:///home/jake/Developer/study/MA/Mathematical-Analysis-%26-Modeling/DEQ/3.%20Linear%20Differential%20Equations/3.3.%20The%20Cauchy-Euler%20Equations/Lessons/3.3.1.%20Cauchy-Euler%20Equations-%20Characteristic%20Equations%20With%20Distinct%20Real%20Roots.md>); [Cauchy-Euler Equations: Characteristic Equations With Repeated Roots](<file:///home/jake/Developer/study/MA/Mathematical-Analysis-%26-Modeling/DEQ/3.%20Linear%20Differential%20Equations/3.3.%20The%20Cauchy-Euler%20Equations/Lessons/3.3.2.%20Cauchy-Euler%20Equations-%20Characteristic%20Equations%20With%20Repeated%20Roots.md>); [Cauchy-Euler Equations: Characteristic Equations With Complex Roots](<file:///home/jake/Developer/study/MA/Mathematical-Analysis-%26-Modeling/DEQ/3.%20Linear%20Differential%20Equations/3.3.%20The%20Cauchy-Euler%20Equations/Lessons/3.3.3.%20Cauchy-Euler%20Equations-%20Characteristic%20Equations%20With%20Complex%20Roots.md>).\n**Prerequisites:** No additional prerequisite links needed." :tutorial/id #uuid "0f97993b-9e3a-5621-807f-4cb2646d6ab5" :tutorial/title "Assignment information"}] [:question/id #uuid "b047f39b-2ef1-5e24-bf80-8e6219b98d6e" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761937 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70969c63-a286-5eba-b680-97393e34db71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8r^2-7r+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761931}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761931} :answer-field/id #uuid "46ee357d-6deb-55bb-9203-ca8e853c0a9d" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761928}] :question/id #uuid "b047f39b-2ef1-5e24-bf80-8e6219b98d6e" :question/problem "Source question: PDF p. 3, Problem 3.\n\nFind the characteristic polynomial of the homogeneous second-order linear equation with constant coefficients\n$$8y''-7y'+5y=0.$$\n$p(r)=$ {{answer}}." :question/worked-solution "Substituting $y=e^{rx}$ yields $(8r^2-7r+5)e^{rx}=0$. The characteristic polynomial is $p(r)=8r^2-7r+5$; keep the coefficients and signs from the ODE."}] [:question/id #uuid "b4c3214b-c497-5260-8ba7-b9f1e86564ab" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761938 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8be5a4b-8f6f-5f0f-bb60-f7d86be1cb5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761957}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761957} :answer-field/id #uuid "24cb2bff-7b34-5c8b-a442-93558a259094" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761918}] :question/id #uuid "b4c3214b-c497-5260-8ba7-b9f1e86564ab" :question/problem "Source question: PDF p. 3, Problem 5.\n\nThe homogeneous second-order linear equation $y''-4y'+4y=0$ has characteristic polynomial $p(r)=r^2-4r+4$. Find the solution(s) to $p(r)=0$.\n$r=$ {{answer}}." :question/worked-solution "Factor $r^2-4r+4=(r-2)^2$. Therefore $r=2$ is the only distinct root, with multiplicity two. Substitution gives $4-8+4=0$."}] [:question/id #uuid "bf0095af-d1b1-55db-80a5-342a259f7a9a" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761942 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "515a31fb-6b92-5b6e-99cc-795571b5f37f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "C_1\\cos(6x)+C_2\\sin(6x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761930}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761930} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0aa2f13-4294-5d93-9d08-8bdf01e05184" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761954}] :question/id #uuid "bf0095af-d1b1-55db-80a5-342a259f7a9a" :question/problem "Source question: PDF p. 4, Problem 8.\n\nThe characteristic equation $r^2+36=0$ for $y''+36y=0$ has solutions $r=\\pm6i$. Find the general solution to\n$$y''+36y=0.$$\n$y(x)=$ {{answer}}." :question/worked-solution "For roots $\\lambda\\pm i\\omega$, the real solution is $e^{\\lambda x}(C_1\\cos(\\omega x)+C_2\\sin(\\omega x))$. Here $\\lambda=0$ and $\\omega=6$, so the exponential factor is $1$. Twice differentiating either trigonometric term multiplies it by $-36$, which verifies the ODE."}] [:question/id #uuid "13cc7856-c9e2-5862-a4fb-c5632cf22327" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761912 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe02b237-2431-5d88-9593-9b012c543e86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pm\\sqrt{17}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761958}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761958} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4f6c89e-3b4d-5d85-8013-c6ed2e746df7" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761956}] :question/id #uuid "13cc7856-c9e2-5862-a4fb-c5632cf22327" :question/problem "Source question: PDF p. 5, Problem 9.\n\nThe indicial polynomial for\n$$2x^2y''-3xy'+y=0$$\nis $p(r)=2r(r-1)-3r+1$. Find the solutions $r$ to the indicial equation $p(r)=0$.\n$r=$ {{answer}}." :question/worked-solution "Expand first: $2r(r-1)-3r+1=2r^2-5r+1$. The quadratic formula gives $r=(5\\pm\\sqrt{25-8})/4=(5\\pm\\sqrt{17})/4$. Their sum is $5/2$ and product is $1/2$, agreeing with the coefficients of the quadratic."}] [:question/id #uuid "18d87466-3553-57d8-a0e9-964f47e356ae" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761913 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf2a4057-639d-57a9-9518-d7e6dbcb1a17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "For $y_1=e^{3x}$ and $y_2=xe^{3x}$,\n$$y_1'=3e^{3x},\\qquad y_2'=(1+3x)e^{3x}.$$\nHence\n$$W=e^{3x}(1+3x)e^{3x}-(3e^{3x})(xe^{3x})=e^{6x}>0.$$\nBoth functions solve the ODE: $y_1''=9e^{3x}$ gives $(9-18+9)e^{3x}=0$, and $y_2''=(6+9x)e^{3x}$ gives $[(6+9x)-6(1+3x)+9x]e^{3x}=0$.\nThe coefficients are continuous on $(0,\\infty)$, and the two solutions have nonzero Wronskian there. They therefore form a fundamental set on the required interval." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761947}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761947} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a978cf8-fde7-5977-b5dd-b989254a621b" :answer-field/key "response" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408167} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761914}] :question/id #uuid "18d87466-3553-57d8-a0e9-964f47e356ae" :question/problem "Source question: PDF p. 2, Problem 2.\n\nThe set of functions $\\{e^{3x},xe^{3x}\\}$ solve\n$$y''-6y'+9y=0.$$\nShow that $\\{e^{3x},xe^{3x}\\}$ form a fundamental set of solutions for $0<x<\\infty$. Your solution must include a Wronskian calculation." :question/worked-solution "Apply the product rule to $xe^{3x}$; its derivative contains both $e^{3x}$ and $3xe^{3x}$. The $3xe^{6x}$ terms then cancel in the Wronskian."}] [:question/id #uuid "228f22c4-9d80-5d95-bd19-73b3f5bad258" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761917 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df1ec775-25c8-5177-a130-e1547608717a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "C_1x+C_2x^{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761950}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761950} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a6be089-872c-5fc5-8863-8711b543e132" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761923}] :question/id #uuid "228f22c4-9d80-5d95-bd19-73b3f5bad258" :question/problem "Source question: PDF p. 5, Problem 10.\n\nThe source states that the indicial equation\n$$r^2+2r-3=0$$\nfor the Euler equation\n$$y''+3xy'-3y=0$$\nhas solutions $r_1=-1$ and $r_2=3$. Find the general solution to\n$$x^2y''+3xy'-3y=0.$$\n\n**Source conflict:** The first displayed ODE omits the $x^2$ multiplying $y''$, and the stated roots do not solve the printed indicial equation. For the study answer, solve the final displayed ODE on $(0,\\infty)$, using the Euler-theorem interval from page 1.\n\n$y(x)=$ {{answer}}." :question/worked-solution "Substitute $y=x^r$ into the final ODE to get $r(r-1)+3r-3=r^2+2r-3=(r-1)(r+3)=0$. Its actual roots are $1$ and $-3$, so $y=C_1x+C_2x^{-3}$ on $(0,\\infty)$. Each term has residual zero in the final ODE.\n\nThe key follows the erroneous stated roots and gives $C_1x^{-1}+C_2x^3$. These terms have residuals $-4x^{-1}$ and $12x^3$, respectively, so that expression is not the general solution of the final printed equation. It would instead solve $x^2y''-xy'-3y=0$. The original wording is retained above; no instructor correction has been assumed."}] [:question/id #uuid "32211eb5-452e-50c2-87eb-6641cda913b9" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761924 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d81e193a-6a5e-5409-b9f7-d18f0dfa6be1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3r^2+7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761949}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761949} :answer-field/id #uuid "8865409b-88f0-570a-95f6-4c61d06a183a" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761933}] :question/id #uuid "32211eb5-452e-50c2-87eb-6641cda913b9" :question/problem "Source question: PDF p. 3, Problem 4.\n\nFind the characteristic polynomial of the homogeneous second-order linear equation with constant coefficients\n$$-3y''+7y=0.$$\n$p(r)=$ {{answer}}." :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of $y''$, $y'$, and $y$ are $-3$, $0$, and $7$. Thus $p(r)=-3r^2+0r+7=-3r^2+7$. The absence of a $y'$ term gives a zero linear coefficient."}] [:question/id #uuid "370002b3-18c2-59d0-9262-89109225fc9c" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761926 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91b2c46b-2c16-5cc2-8f7c-e9becf6bf9c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pm3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761934}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761934} :answer-field/id #uuid "2673b996-840f-5b02-be16-eecc2f38da86" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761920}] :question/id #uuid "370002b3-18c2-59d0-9262-89109225fc9c" :question/problem "Source question: PDF p. 3, Problem 6.\n\nThe homogeneous second-order linear equation $y''-9y=0$ has characteristic polynomial $p(r)=r^2-9$. Find the solution(s) to $p(r)=0$.\n$r=$ {{answer}}." :question/worked-solution "Factor $r^2-9=(r-3)(r+3)$; either factor may be zero. The roots are $3$ and $-3$, written $\\pm3$. Both square to $9$."}] [:question/id #uuid "3b51ca7a-7572-534e-b8b8-82d1a8af6ccb" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761927 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d27c13c3-fb87-56cd-b9aa-d267ea5e9f8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The requested conclusion is false for the printed ODE. With $y_1=x^{-3}$ and $y_2=x$, the derivatives are $y_1'=-3x^{-4}$ and $y_2'=1$, so\n$$W=y_1y_2'-y_1'y_2=x^{-3}-(-3x^{-4})x=4x^{-3}\\ne0\\quad(x>0).$$\nThus the functions are linearly independent. But for $L[y]=x^2y''+4xy'-y$,\n$$L[x^{-3}]=12x^{-3}-12x^{-3}-x^{-3}=-x^{-3},\\qquad L[x]=4x-x=3x.$$\nNeither function solves the printed equation, so they cannot be its fundamental set.\n\nThe key instead prints $x^2y''+3xy'-y=0$; its residuals are $2x^{-3}$ and $2x$, so that version also fails. The equation $x^2y''+3xy'-3y=0$ does have these two solutions, and the same nonzero Wronskian proves they form a fundamental set on $(0,\\infty)$. This last equation is an authored possible correction, not a confirmed instructor correction." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761948}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761948} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd0ff44b-2295-53ff-a2de-b5a8b8c914e1" :answer-field/key "response" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408167} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761946}] :question/id #uuid "3b51ca7a-7572-534e-b8b8-82d1a8af6ccb" :question/problem "Source question: PDF p. 2, Problem 1.\n\nThe set of functions $\\{x^{-3},x\\}$ solve\n$$x^2y''+4xy'-y=0.$$\nShow that $\\{x^{-3},x\\}$ form a fundamental set of solutions for $0<x<\\infty$. Your solution must include a Wronskian calculation.\n\n**Source conflict:** The worksheet and key print different equations here. The assertion is inconsistent with both printed versions. Preserve the requested Wronskian work and explain what the calculation actually establishes." :question/worked-solution "A nonzero Wronskian proves independence; membership in the ODE's solution set must also hold. The key's Wronskian is correct, but it does not establish the claimed solution property. The instructor's intended correction remains unconfirmed."}] [:question/id #uuid "7523b767-f708-50e1-8742-fd8935f8683a" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761932 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f455c77f-17ac-53fb-a80c-ab067f59d9a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(C_1+C_2x)e^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761955}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761955} :answer-field/id #uuid "09d9e7ad-81d9-5f7d-a366-2534e1078648" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761907}] :question/id #uuid "7523b767-f708-50e1-8742-fd8935f8683a" :question/problem "Source question: PDF p. 4, Problem 7.\n\nThe characteristic equation $r^2-4r+4=0$ for $y''-4y'+4y=0$ has a single real solution $r=2$. Find the general solution to\n$$y''-4y'+4y=0.$$\n$y(x)=$ {{answer}}." :question/worked-solution "The root $r=2$ is repeated. Two independent solutions are $e^{2x}$ and $xe^{2x}$, so $y=(C_1+C_2x)e^{2x}$, with arbitrary constants. Their Wronskian is $e^{4x}\\ne0$, and each has residual zero in $y''-4y'+4y$. Repeating $e^{2x}$ without the factor $x$ would provide only one independent solution."}] [:answer-field/id #uuid "8865409b-88f0-570a-95f6-4c61d06a183a" {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d81e193a-6a5e-5409-b9f7-d18f0dfa6be1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3r^2+7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761949}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761949} :answer-field/id #uuid "8865409b-88f0-570a-95f6-4c61d06a183a" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761933}] [:answer-field/id #uuid "cd0ff44b-2295-53ff-a2de-b5a8b8c914e1" {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d27c13c3-fb87-56cd-b9aa-d267ea5e9f8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The requested conclusion is false for the printed ODE. With $y_1=x^{-3}$ and $y_2=x$, the derivatives are $y_1'=-3x^{-4}$ and $y_2'=1$, so\n$$W=y_1y_2'-y_1'y_2=x^{-3}-(-3x^{-4})x=4x^{-3}\\ne0\\quad(x>0).$$\nThus the functions are linearly independent. But for $L[y]=x^2y''+4xy'-y$,\n$$L[x^{-3}]=12x^{-3}-12x^{-3}-x^{-3}=-x^{-3},\\qquad L[x]=4x-x=3x.$$\nNeither function solves the printed equation, so they cannot be its fundamental set.\n\nThe key instead prints $x^2y''+3xy'-y=0$; its residuals are $2x^{-3}$ and $2x$, so that version also fails. The equation $x^2y''+3xy'-3y=0$ does have these two solutions, and the same nonzero Wronskian proves they form a fundamental set on $(0,\\infty)$. This last equation is an authored possible correction, not a confirmed instructor correction." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761948}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761948} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd0ff44b-2295-53ff-a2de-b5a8b8c914e1" :answer-field/key "response" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408167} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761946}] [:answer-field/id #uuid "f0aa2f13-4294-5d93-9d08-8bdf01e05184" {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "515a31fb-6b92-5b6e-99cc-795571b5f37f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "C_1\\cos(6x)+C_2\\sin(6x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761930}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761930} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0aa2f13-4294-5d93-9d08-8bdf01e05184" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761954}] [:answer-field/id #uuid "f4f6c89e-3b4d-5d85-8013-c6ed2e746df7" {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe02b237-2431-5d88-9593-9b012c543e86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pm\\sqrt{17}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761958}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761958} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4f6c89e-3b4d-5d85-8013-c6ed2e746df7" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761956}] [:answer-field/id #uuid "09d9e7ad-81d9-5f7d-a366-2534e1078648" {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f455c77f-17ac-53fb-a80c-ab067f59d9a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(C_1+C_2x)e^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761955}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761955} :answer-field/id #uuid "09d9e7ad-81d9-5f7d-a366-2534e1078648" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761907}] [:answer-field/id #uuid "1a978cf8-fde7-5977-b5dd-b989254a621b" {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf2a4057-639d-57a9-9518-d7e6dbcb1a17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "For $y_1=e^{3x}$ and $y_2=xe^{3x}$,\n$$y_1'=3e^{3x},\\qquad y_2'=(1+3x)e^{3x}.$$\nHence\n$$W=e^{3x}(1+3x)e^{3x}-(3e^{3x})(xe^{3x})=e^{6x}>0.$$\nBoth functions solve the ODE: $y_1''=9e^{3x}$ gives $(9-18+9)e^{3x}=0$, and $y_2''=(6+9x)e^{3x}$ gives $[(6+9x)-6(1+3x)+9x]e^{3x}=0$.\nThe coefficients are continuous on $(0,\\infty)$, and the two solutions have nonzero Wronskian there. They therefore form a fundamental set on the required interval." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761947}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761947} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a978cf8-fde7-5977-b5dd-b989254a621b" :answer-field/key "response" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408167} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761914}] [:answer-field/id #uuid "24cb2bff-7b34-5c8b-a442-93558a259094" {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8be5a4b-8f6f-5f0f-bb60-f7d86be1cb5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761957}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761957} :answer-field/id #uuid "24cb2bff-7b34-5c8b-a442-93558a259094" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761918}] [:answer-field/id #uuid "2673b996-840f-5b02-be16-eecc2f38da86" {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91b2c46b-2c16-5cc2-8f7c-e9becf6bf9c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pm3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761934}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761934} :answer-field/id #uuid "2673b996-840f-5b02-be16-eecc2f38da86" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761920}] [:answer-field/id #uuid "2a6be089-872c-5fc5-8863-8711b543e132" {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df1ec775-25c8-5177-a130-e1547608717a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "C_1x+C_2x^{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761950}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761950} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a6be089-872c-5fc5-8863-8711b543e132" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761923}] [:answer-field/id #uuid "46ee357d-6deb-55bb-9203-ca8e853c0a9d" {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70969c63-a286-5eba-b680-97393e34db71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8r^2-7r+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761931}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761931} :answer-field/id #uuid "46ee357d-6deb-55bb-9203-ca8e853c0a9d" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761928}] [:answer/id #uuid "91b2c46b-2c16-5cc2-8f7c-e9becf6bf9c2" {:answer/id #uuid "91b2c46b-2c16-5cc2-8f7c-e9becf6bf9c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pm3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761934}] [:answer/id #uuid "cf2a4057-639d-57a9-9518-d7e6dbcb1a17" {:answer/id #uuid "cf2a4057-639d-57a9-9518-d7e6dbcb1a17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "For $y_1=e^{3x}$ and $y_2=xe^{3x}$,\n$$y_1'=3e^{3x},\\qquad y_2'=(1+3x)e^{3x}.$$\nHence\n$$W=e^{3x}(1+3x)e^{3x}-(3e^{3x})(xe^{3x})=e^{6x}>0.$$\nBoth functions solve the ODE: $y_1''=9e^{3x}$ gives $(9-18+9)e^{3x}=0$, and $y_2''=(6+9x)e^{3x}$ gives $[(6+9x)-6(1+3x)+9x]e^{3x}=0$.\nThe coefficients are continuous on $(0,\\infty)$, and the two solutions have nonzero Wronskian there. They therefore form a fundamental set on the required interval." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761947}] [:answer/id #uuid "d27c13c3-fb87-56cd-b9aa-d267ea5e9f8f" {:answer/id #uuid "d27c13c3-fb87-56cd-b9aa-d267ea5e9f8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The requested conclusion is false for the printed ODE. With $y_1=x^{-3}$ and $y_2=x$, the derivatives are $y_1'=-3x^{-4}$ and $y_2'=1$, so\n$$W=y_1y_2'-y_1'y_2=x^{-3}-(-3x^{-4})x=4x^{-3}\\ne0\\quad(x>0).$$\nThus the functions are linearly independent. But for $L[y]=x^2y''+4xy'-y$,\n$$L[x^{-3}]=12x^{-3}-12x^{-3}-x^{-3}=-x^{-3},\\qquad L[x]=4x-x=3x.$$\nNeither function solves the printed equation, so they cannot be its fundamental set.\n\nThe key instead prints $x^2y''+3xy'-y=0$; its residuals are $2x^{-3}$ and $2x$, so that version also fails. The equation $x^2y''+3xy'-3y=0$ does have these two solutions, and the same nonzero Wronskian proves they form a fundamental set on $(0,\\infty)$. This last equation is an authored possible correction, not a confirmed instructor correction." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761948}] [:answer/id #uuid "d81e193a-6a5e-5409-b9f7-d18f0dfa6be1" {:answer/id #uuid "d81e193a-6a5e-5409-b9f7-d18f0dfa6be1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3r^2+7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761949}] [:answer/id #uuid "df1ec775-25c8-5177-a130-e1547608717a" {:answer/id #uuid "df1ec775-25c8-5177-a130-e1547608717a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "C_1x+C_2x^{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761950}] [:answer/id #uuid "f455c77f-17ac-53fb-a80c-ab067f59d9a1" {:answer/id #uuid "f455c77f-17ac-53fb-a80c-ab067f59d9a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(C_1+C_2x)e^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761955}] [:answer/id #uuid "f8be5a4b-8f6f-5f0f-bb60-f7d86be1cb5b" {:answer/id #uuid "f8be5a4b-8f6f-5f0f-bb60-f7d86be1cb5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761957}] [:answer/id #uuid "fe02b237-2431-5d88-9593-9b012c543e86" {:answer/id #uuid "fe02b237-2431-5d88-9593-9b012c543e86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pm\\sqrt{17}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761958}] [:answer/id #uuid "515a31fb-6b92-5b6e-99cc-795571b5f37f" {:answer/id #uuid "515a31fb-6b92-5b6e-99cc-795571b5f37f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "C_1\\cos(6x)+C_2\\sin(6x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761930}] [:answer/id #uuid "70969c63-a286-5eba-b680-97393e34db71" {:answer/id #uuid "70969c63-a286-5eba-b680-97393e34db71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8r^2-7r+5" :db/id #edb/ref 1